{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2657","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2657","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2657\",\"parent_revision\":\"dada22e6f1c52a0581f46a341db68b38777f2779\",\"root_revision\":\"bcfcdf77d6fbdc2b43ef8dcfcf59963a79bc15789753c69339ad78a91bf03f3a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen a backlog item is created from a session observation, the source log file is recorded only as free text in the `source` field or Resources section. There is no structured mechanism to attach a session log file as evidence, no enforcement that the file was read before the item was created, and no way to distinguish \\\"observed in this log\\\" from \\\"recalled from memory.\\\"\\n\\nThis caused a concrete failure today: a backlog item (#2656) was created citing a session log and a 35-day-old memory entry as joint sources. The grooming swarm treated both as equally current. The memory-sourced claim (Gap 2) was never verified against the actual session log, resulting in a full grooming and planning cycle built on unverified stale data.\\n\\nThe backlog has no \\\"evidence files\\\" field \u2014 only a free-text `source` field and a free-text Resources section. Neither enforces that the referenced log was read or that its content was verified against the item description before the item was written.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **backlog items have no structured evidence attachment for session logs** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 dh-with-bd-example.txt (2026-06-19), issue #2656 post-mortem\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-19\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-20)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n- Goal: Add structured evidence attachment for session log files to backlog items, with an agent-enforced distinction between log-observed claims and memory-recalled claims, preventing unverified stale data from entering the grooming and planning pipeline.\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - Backlog item `source` field exists as free-text string on `BacklogItemMetadata` (`backlog_core/models.py:715`)\\n  - `backlog_add` MCP tool accepts `source` as a free-text parameter (defaults to `\\\"Not specified\\\"`)\\n  - `create-backlog-item` workflow (`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md`) sets `source` at creation time\\n  - The concrete failure is documented: #2656 grooming swarm treated a 35-day-old memory entry as equally current as a log file, producing a grooming/planning cycle on unverified stale data\\n  - `docs/sdlc-layers/layer-0/evidence-discipline.md` defines the valid/invalid evidence boundary (training-data recall = invalid)\\n  - `backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` defines the `Output / Evidence` section for groomed items \u2014 but this only documents evidence after grooming, not at creation time\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - The schema change needed: `BacklogItemMetadata` would need an `evidence_files` field (list of paths) alongside the existing `source` string \u2014 derivable by reading `models.py` class definition\\n  - The workflow change needed: `create-backlog-item` guardrail that requires source logs to be read before the item is written \u2014 derivable from the existing `start.md` field-derivation step\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Enforcement mechanism scope: does \\\"enforcement\\\" mean (1) a schema field that agents are instructed to populate, (2) a skill-level guardrail rule in `create/start.md` that blocks writing until the log is read, or (3) an MCP-level validation that rejects items citing a log path that has not been accessed? This is a design decision with meaningfully different implementation surfaces.\\n  - Whether `evidence_files` belongs in frontmatter YAML (persisted, queryable) or only in the item body (free-text prose) \u2014 affects `backlog_core/models.py`, `parsing.py`, and the GitHub sync surface.\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Feature (new structured field + creation-time guardrail)\\n- Behavioral boundary: The creation workflow for a backlog item (`create-backlog-item` / `work-backlog-item create`) \u2014 specifically the moment when a source reference is recorded\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Given a session log file as source, the creation workflow must require the log to be read before the item body is written; the item must record the log path as a structured evidence reference; agents downstream (fact-checker, groomer) must be able to distinguish log-sourced claims from memory-recalled claims\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Create a backlog item with `source: \\\"Session observation \u2014 some-log.txt (2026-06-19), 35-day-old memory\\\"` via `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_add`\\n2. Observe that both sources are stored identically as a single free-text string\\n3. Spawn a groomer swarm on the resulting item\\n4. Observe that groomer treats the memory-recalled claim and the log-sourced claim as equally valid without reading the log\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- Concrete failure: Backlog item #2656 was created citing `dh-with-bd-example.txt` (2026-06-19) and a 35-day-old memory entry as joint sources. The grooming swarm's fact-checker processed both without reading the log file. Gap 2 (\\\"BeadsBackend.try_get_github() AttributeError in subagent context\\\") originated from the 35-day-old memory and was never verified against the actual session log, resulting in a full grooming and planning cycle on unverified stale data.\\n- Evidence location: Backlog item #2656, sections `[35] Claim 2` and `[36] Claim 3` in fact-check results; Context section cites `dh-with-bd-example.txt (2026-06-19)` as source\\n- Current schema evidence: `backlog_core/models.py:715` \u2014 `source: str = \\\"Not specified\\\"` \u2014 single free-text string, no structure, no path list, no read-verification flag\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n6/10 \u2014 Prevents a recurring class of grooming failures (memory-sourced claims entering planning). P2 is appropriate: the failure has a workaround (manually reading the log before writing the item), but the lack of enforcement means the failure will recur. No downstream blocking.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Grooming correctness when items are created from session observations with mixed source provenance\\n- Bottleneck: The fact-check phase cannot distinguish a log-observed claim from a memory-recalled claim because both are encoded as free text; the fact-checker has no structured path to read\\n- Affected agents: `@dh:backlog-item-groomer`, `@dh:fact-checker`, `@dh:rtica-assessor`\\n- Recurring pattern: Any session where an agent creates a backlog item from a log file plus memory produces unverified stale data in the groomed item without detection\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Grooming swarms gain a structured evidence path to read before processing claims, reducing unverified stale data in acceptance criteria and planning\\n- The fact-checker can cite the log file as primary source, distinguishing it from training-data recall\\n- The creation workflow enforces the evidence-discipline rule (`evidence-discipline.md`) at item creation time, not only at grooming time\\n- Backlog items become auditable: the evidence path is queryable, not buried in prose\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a session log file is identified as the source of a backlog item:\\n\\n1. The `create-backlog-item` workflow (or equivalent agent) reads the log file before writing the item body\\n2. The item records the log file path in a structured `evidence_files` field (or equivalent), separate from the free-text `source` field\\n3. The groomer agent receives the evidence file path as a structured input and reads it before processing claims\\n4. The fact-checker agent can distinguish claims prefixed with a log file path (verifiable by reading) from claims with no file path (memory-recalled, requires web search or codebase verification)\\n5. A backlog item with no evidence file attached but a `source` referencing a session log is flagged as incomplete during grooming\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nThe target state when done:\\n\\n- `BacklogItemMetadata` has an optional `evidence_files: list[str]` field (or equivalent structured field) alongside the existing `source: str` field\\n- `backlog_add` MCP tool accepts `evidence_files` as an optional list parameter\\n- `create-backlog-item` workflow includes a guardrail step: when `source` references a file path, the agent must read that file before writing the item\\n- The `groom-backlog-item` workflow passes `evidence_files` to the groomer agent, which reads each file before processing claims from it\\n- The groomed item's `Output / Evidence` section distinguishes log-sourced evidence (file path cited, content read) from memory-recalled evidence (flagged for fact-check)\\n- The `fact-checker` agent receives structured evidence paths and can verify claims against them directly\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given `backlog_add(source=\\\"session-log.txt\\\", evidence_files=[\\\"path/to/session-log.txt\\\"])`, the resulting item's frontmatter contains a structured `evidence_files` list, not only a free-text `source` string\\n2. Given a `create-backlog-item` invocation where `source` references a file path, the creation workflow reads that file before the item is written \u2014 observable by the presence of a `Read` tool call on the file in the session transcript\\n3. Given a grooming swarm on an item with `evidence_files` populated, the groomer agent receives each file path, reads it, and cites it in the `Output / Evidence` section \u2014 distinguishable from items where no evidence file is present\\n4. Given an item where `source` references a session log but `evidence_files` is empty, the groom workflow flags this as incomplete in the `Blockers` section rather than silently accepting it\\n5. Given two claims in the same backlog item \u2014 one from a log file (path in `evidence_files`) and one from memory (no path) \u2014 the fact-checker treats the memory claim as requiring verification and the log-sourced claim as primary-source evidence\\n6. Reproduce the #2656 failure scenario: create an item citing a log file and a memory entry; the creation workflow reads the log file; the groomer surfaces the memory-sourced claim for fact-checking; the planning cycle is not started on the memory-sourced claim until it is verified\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- Should `evidence_files` be a first-class field in `BacklogItemMetadata` (persisted to YAML frontmatter, queryable via `backlog_list`) or a prose convention in the item body (simpler, no schema change)?\\n- What is the desired enforcement level at creation time: (a) a skill-level guardrail instruction that agents are told to follow, (b) a required field that causes `backlog_add` to warn when `source` names a file but `evidence_files` is empty, or (c) hard enforcement that rejects the `backlog_add` call if the file path exists but has not been read?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 `BacklogItemMetadata.source` field (line 715) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 `backlog_add` source parameter (line 1128) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` \u2014 `source` field derivation |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/sdlc-layers/layer-0/evidence-discipline.md` \u2014 valid/invalid evidence boundary |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` \u2014 `Output / Evidence` section schema |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/references/item-schema.md` \u2014 `BacklogItemMetadata` field ownership table |\\n| Agent | `@dh:backlog-item-groomer` \u2014 receives source context; needs evidence_files input |\\n| Agent | `@dh:fact-checker` \u2014 needs structured evidence paths to distinguish source types |\\n| Backlog | #2656 \u2014 concrete failure instance (Gap 2 from stale memory source) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 the `source` field and creation workflow already exist; this is an additive change\\n- Blocks: Potential dependency from any item that improves grooming quality; `@dh:fact-checker` behavioral improvements that rely on structured source provenance\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Design decision required: enforcement mechanism scope (schema field only, skill-level guardrail, or MCP-level validation) \u2014 see MISSING in RT-ICA Assessment above\\n- Design decision required: whether `evidence_files` is a frontmatter YAML field (persisted, queryable) or only a body prose convention \u2014 this determines whether `backlog_core/models.py`, `parsing.py`, and GitHub sync are affected\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The schema change (`BacklogItemMetadata`, `backlog_add`) is straightforward. The workflow guardrail in `create/start.md` and the groomer agent prompt update are prose changes. The fact-checker behavioral update requires verifying the agent reads the `evidence_files` input. No new infrastructure is needed.\\n\\n### Groomed (2026-06-20)\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-20T08:13:05.328782Z</sub>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n**Rationale**: The creation workflow has no gate that requires source log files to be read before the item is written. The absence of this gate allows memory-recalled claims to enter the backlog with the same source weight as log-verified claims.\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys\\n**Scenario Target**: Backlog item created from session log + memory \u2192 grooming swarm treats both sources equally \u2192 planning cycle built on unverified stale data \u2192 incorrect implementation scope\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys\\n**Classification**: missing-guardrail\\n\\n#### Evidence Chain\\n\\n1. CLAIM: Backlog item #2656 contained a claim (Gap 2) sourced from a 35-day-old memory entry, not from the cited session log\\n   EVIDENCE: #2656 Context section cites `dh-with-bd-example.txt (2026-06-19)` as source; fact-check Claim 2 (`try_get_github` AttributeError) was sourced from memory without reading the log\\n   VERIFIED: yes\\n   DEPENDS ON: none (symptom)\\n\\n2. CLAIM: The grooming swarm processed the memory-sourced claim without reading the session log file\\n   EVIDENCE: `@dh:fact-checker` has no input field for file paths; the `source` field passed to the groomer is free text; no tool call on `dh-with-bd-example.txt` appeared in the grooming transcript\\n   VERIFIED: yes\\n   DEPENDS ON: 1\\n\\n3. CLAIM: The `backlog_add` tool records `source` as a single free-text string with no structured file path\\n   EVIDENCE: `backlog_core/models.py:715` \u2014 `source: str = \\\"Not specified\\\"` \u2014 confirmed by Grep\\n   VERIFIED: yes\\n   DEPENDS ON: 2\\n\\n4. CLAIM: There is no step in the creation workflow that requires reading a referenced source file before writing the item\\n   EVIDENCE: `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` \u2014 `source` field is set from user input without a read-before-write gate\\n   VERIFIED: yes\\n   DEPENDS ON: 3\\n\\n**Root Cause**: The backlog item creation workflow records the source as free text with no structured file reference and no enforcement that the source file was read, allowing memory-recalled claims to enter the item with the same provenance weight as log-verified claims.\\n**Scenario Target**: Backlog item created from session log + memory \u2192 no read-before-write gate \u2192 grooming swarm cannot distinguish evidence types \u2192 planning cycle on unverified stale data\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"fb50ff0b097e00b336082309fd92c8aad8070d89fa5e9ab26052be76a8d0e0bf\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPnHfvA\"}"}